# Figma → Cursor workflow

Cursor does not connect to Figma directly. To have Cursor use your Figma design, put the design into the repo and reference it in rules.

## 1. Export design tokens from Figma

- **Figma Variables**: In Figma, define Variables (colors, spacing) and export them (e.g. via plugin “Design Tokens” or “Style Dictionary” or “Tokens Studio”).
- **Or manual**: From Figma Inspect, copy colors/spacing into a file in the project.

Add or update tokens in:

- **`tailwind.config.js`** – in `theme.extend.colors` (and optionally `spacing`, `borderRadius`, etc.). The project already uses `rota.*` and `charcoal` / `offwhite` / etc.
- Optionally **`src/styles/theme.css`** or a `design-tokens.json` if you prefer a single source.

Keep one source of truth (e.g. “Figma → tailwind”) and document it here or in a short README.

## 2. Document layout and components in rules

For specific screens or components (e.g. a new modal, a card):

- In Figma: note spacing, typography, colors, border radius.
- In this repo: add or update a **visual reference rule** in `.cursor/rules/` (like `confirm-dialog-visual-reference.mdc` or `shunter-card-visual-reference.mdc`): tables with Property | Value (Tailwind classes or token names).
- Reference that rule in the rule description so Cursor knows when to use it (e.g. “use when building X”).

Then Cursor will use **your** layout and components, not generic ones.

## 3. Summary

| Goal | Action |
|------|--------|
| Cursor uses your colors/spacing | Put tokens in `tailwind.config.js` (or theme file); use them in components. |
| Cursor uses your components/layout | Document in `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` (visual reference rules). |
| New Figma design | Export tokens → update Tailwind/theme; write or update a visual-reference rule for that screen/component. |

No native “Figma plugin for Cursor” is required: **Figma → export to repo → Cursor reads repo**.
